WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.717
Picture a laundromat after closing. The only light comes from neon tubes, pink on one wall and blue on the other.

00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:16.707
The pink tube is on its last legs. Every time it stutters, the floor changes colour, and the shadows behind the washers swing around with it.

00:00:17.057 --> 00:00:24.545
Look at one of those shadows. Right against the washer its edge is crisp. Further down the aisle it smears into a soft blur.

00:00:24.895 --> 00:00:32.831
That blurry rim is called the penumbra. Getting a computer to draw it, quickly and every frame, is the problem we're going to work through.

00:00:34.031 --> 00:00:37.829
Here one of the drums has come loose and is rolling down the aisle.

00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:46.221
Watch its shadow. Near the drum the edge stays sharp, a little further out it goes soft, and it keeps doing that wherever the drum goes.

00:00:46.571 --> 00:00:56.534
The obvious way to draw this is to stand on every spot of the floor and fire a few hundred rays across the whole room. It looks right, but it's painfully slow.

00:00:57.734 --> 00:01:05.521
So we measured what the shadow actually needs, standing at five spots, from right beside the drum to a good way down the aisle.

00:01:05.871 --> 00:01:15.407
Close to the drum the light changes quickly as you move, so the sample points have to be packed tight. The spacing should be no bigger than the distance to the drum.

00:01:15.757 --> 00:01:25.037
Far away it flips. Positions can spread out, but you need to look in more directions. The angle between rays should be no bigger than one over that distance.

00:01:25.387 --> 00:01:41.621
The spacing part held up. The usable spacing roughly doubled as we walked away. The angle part never showed up, because the neon around the whole room, not this one drum, decides how many directions you need. So that check failed, and we left it failed.

00:01:42.821 --> 00:01:46.533
Radiance cascades build that trade-off into the data itself.

00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:54.435
The bottom layer has lots of probes packed close together, and each one looks in only four directions, over a short distance.

00:01:54.785 --> 00:02:04.727
Every layer up, the probes sit twice as far apart, so there are a quarter as many. Each one looks in four times as many directions, and four times as far.

00:02:05.077 --> 00:02:11.327
A quarter of the probes times four times the rays comes out even, so every layer costs about the same.

00:02:11.677 --> 00:02:20.616
Here the layers arrive from the top down. The picture starts as a blurry guess, and the sharp detail comes in with the close-range layers at the end.

00:02:20.966 --> 00:02:29.009
Keep an eye on the drums. The soft glow is there early, and the crisp edges only show up once the bottom layers have joined in.

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:38.635
To stack the layers we need one rule for joining them. Each ray only covers a stretch of distance, from where it starts to where it stops.

00:02:38.985 --> 00:02:48.308
Along that stretch it either hits something and brings back that surface's light, or it passes through empty space and lets whatever is behind it shine through.

00:02:48.658 --> 00:02:57.682
Written out, the light over the whole distance is the light from the near stretch, plus how much the near stretch lets through, times the light from the far stretch.

00:02:58.032 --> 00:03:03.344
And how much the whole thing lets through is just the two see-through amounts multiplied together.

00:03:04.544 --> 00:03:07.168
That rule is really just looking through a window.

00:03:07.518 --> 00:03:11.422
If the near stretch hits a wall, nothing from further out gets through.

00:03:11.772 --> 00:03:18.961
If the near stretch is clear, you see whatever the next layer up already found, as if you had fired one very long ray.

00:03:19.311 --> 00:03:30.213
In this shot, the coloured lines are one probe's rays at four different layers. The short ones near the middle catch the crisp edges. The long ones reaching out catch the soft glow.

00:03:30.563 --> 00:03:35.917
One rule, used at every distance, gives you the sharp contact and the soft aisle together.

00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:44.157
Now the bill. Here the light over the change machine slides along the back wall, and every frame is lit from scratch.

00:03:44.507 --> 00:03:48.582
Our reference fires a few hundred full-length rays from every bit of floor.

00:03:48.932 --> 00:03:58.319
The cascades fire about a hundred and fifty times fewer rays, and short ones at that, so the total marching work drops by more than four hundred times.

00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:09.997
To be straight about speed, these clips were simulated offline on an ordinary processor, at about a quarter of a second per frame. Games run the same idea on the graphics card.

00:04:11.197 --> 00:04:16.935
Now the known weak spot. The layer above is coarser, so each probe blends four neighbours from it.

00:04:17.285 --> 00:04:22.661
If those four sit on both sides of a thin wall, light from one room can sneak into the next.

00:04:23.011 --> 00:04:32.377
We expected the amber closet light to bleed into the aisle. It didn't. Next to the wall the result came out a little darker than the reference, not brighter.

00:04:33.577 --> 00:04:42.643
The usual fix is to trace four short rays, one toward each of those neighbours, join each one on its own, and only blend at the very end.

00:04:42.993 --> 00:04:51.185
Since nothing leaked in the first place, there was nothing here for the fix to clean up. That check failed too, and we're reporting it as it is.

00:04:51.535 --> 00:04:59.706
A cheaper trick is to let the layers overlap a little. We measured it, it came out a bit worse, so we didn't use it for the final picture.

00:05:00.906 --> 00:05:08.885
Some limits. This is one flat floor plan, worked out on the processor, with six layers. It isn't a full three D game engine.

00:05:09.235 --> 00:05:20.541
Path of Exile two ships radiance cascades in screen space, as Alexander Sannikov describes in his paper and his ExileCon talk. That's their result, not ours.

00:05:20.891 --> 00:05:29.147
Even our reference picture carries a little noise of its own. The cascade error is about eight times bigger than that, so the gap is real.

00:05:29.497 --> 00:05:38.372
Final score, eight checks pass and four fail. The cascade image ends up about one sixth off the reference, a touch over the target we set.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:45.695
A soft shadow needs fine detail in position up close, and fine detail in direction far away.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:52.039
Cascades store exactly that, layer by layer, and joining the layers is looking through a window.

00:05:52.389 --> 00:06:00.453
The method comes from Alexander Sannikov, with a later write-up of the blending fix by Osborne and Sannikov. Thanks for watching.

